1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:07,282
Two identical swings. Every
push is exactly the same size.

2
00:00:07,632 --> 00:00:11,267
On the left, one push per swing,
always at the same moment.

3
00:00:11,307 --> 00:00:14,609
On the right, the same
pushes, at random moments.

4
00:00:16,759 --> 00:00:20,343
Same push. Different timing.
A very different result.

5
00:00:20,993 --> 00:00:22,357
This is resonance.

6
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:27,714
Anything that can wiggle
has a natural rhythm:

7
00:00:27,754 --> 00:00:30,559
the beat it keeps when
you pull it and let go.

8
00:00:30,809 --> 00:00:36,347
A swing on 2-metre chains goes back
and forth about once every 2.8 seconds.

9
00:00:37,297 --> 00:00:42,087
Engineers study this with the simplest
wiggler of all: a mass on a spring.

10
00:00:43,237 --> 00:00:46,045
Its natural rhythm depends
on just two things.

11
00:00:46,085 --> 00:00:48,088
A stiffer spring makes it faster.

12
00:00:48,128 --> 00:00:50,297
A heavier mass makes it slower.

13
00:00:51,247 --> 00:00:54,331
Now let's push it, gently,
at a rhythm we choose.

14
00:00:54,881 --> 00:00:57,965
Push slowly, and the mass
simply follows the push.

15
00:00:58,715 --> 00:01:03,381
Push faster, closer to its natural
rhythm, and the motion starts to grow.

16
00:01:03,931 --> 00:01:05,477
Right at its natural rhythm,

17
00:01:05,517 --> 00:01:09,164
the motion is ten times bigger
than a slow push can make it.

18
00:01:09,814 --> 00:01:11,076
Here is the secret:

19
00:01:11,116 --> 00:01:15,874
at resonance, every push goes the
same way the mass is already moving.

20
00:01:15,914 --> 00:01:17,946
So every push adds energy.

21
00:01:18,696 --> 00:01:22,723
Push faster still, and the mass
cannot keep up. It barely moves.

22
00:01:23,673 --> 00:01:26,824
So what stops the motion
growing forever? Damping:

23
00:01:26,864 --> 00:01:32,476
anything that turns motion into heat, like
friction, or the oil in a shock absorber.

24
00:01:32,826 --> 00:01:35,330
Damping sets the height of the peak.

25
00:01:35,370 --> 00:01:38,794
With 5 % damping, the
motion is ten times bigger.

26
00:01:38,834 --> 00:01:41,622
Double the damping, and the peak halves.

27
00:01:41,662 --> 00:01:44,456
At 30 %, only a gentle bump is left.

28
00:01:45,006 --> 00:01:49,082
That is why engineers add damping
to anything that must not shake.

29
00:01:50,232 --> 00:01:51,494
London, June 2000.

30
00:01:51,534 --> 00:01:56,483
The Millennium Bridge opens, and
thousands of people walk across.

31
00:01:56,833 --> 00:02:01,915
Every step pushes the deck a tiny bit
sideways: left, right, left, right.

32
00:02:02,265 --> 00:02:07,072
Once the deck starts to sway, people
shift their feet to keep their balance.

33
00:02:07,112 --> 00:02:11,408
On average, those pushes go the
same way the deck is already moving,

34
00:02:11,448 --> 00:02:13,547
so every step feeds the sway.

35
00:02:14,097 --> 00:02:16,767
Two days later, the bridge is closed.

36
00:02:16,807 --> 00:02:20,722
Engineers fitted dampers
that turn the sway into heat.

37
00:02:20,762 --> 00:02:23,136
In 2002, it reopened, steady.

38
00:02:24,286 --> 00:02:27,106
Taipei 101 goes one step further.

39
00:02:27,146 --> 00:02:32,739
Near the top of the 508-metre
tower hangs a 660-tonne steel ball,

40
00:02:32,779 --> 00:02:35,802
tuned to the tower's own rhythm.

41
00:02:36,252 --> 00:02:40,882
When the wind makes the tower sway, the
ball swings a quarter of a beat behind it,

42
00:02:40,922 --> 00:02:43,280
so its pull works against the motion.

43
00:02:43,830 --> 00:02:46,226
Resonance, used to fight resonance.

44
00:02:47,576 --> 00:02:52,086
Push something at its favourite rhythm,
and tiny pushes make huge movements.

